WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.249
Film Courage: &nbsp;I’m wondering if the two
of you can tell me about how your editor made

00:00:03.249 --> 00:00:07.479
sense of all the footage? &nbsp;I know you spent
like 5 or 6 years with him [Artist and Documentary

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:10.110
Subject Chris Burden] and were very close
to Chris and close to the footage as well.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.770
&nbsp;How did your editor&nbsp;really help shape the
film? Timothy Marrinan: &nbsp;Yeah, it was a really

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:20.390
important process. &nbsp;As you say, we spent
a long time filming with Chris, lots of different

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.829
pieces. &nbsp;We went through his archives, so
there’s a lot of raw material there to piece

00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:31.650
together into a cohesive story. &nbsp; We worked
with two editors, Michael Aaglund and&nbsp;Aaron

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:36.980
Wickenden, who were fantastic editors. &nbsp;And
it was so useful to have them come in with

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:42.250
a little more distance than we had, because
we had been so close to it for so long. &nbsp;So

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:49.140
to get their sort of fresh eyes on the project
and help us figure out what was important

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.780
to go in there and what wasn’t and help&nbsp;shape
that story, they were fantastic and it was

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.330
a really important part of&nbsp;the process of
making the film. Film Courage: &nbsp;How much

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:05.420
footage did you have of Chris over the years?
Timothy Marrinan: &nbsp;I suppose it’s hard

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.670
to say in terms of specific kind of hours
and things, but the film took shape over a

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:15.439
number of years because Chris’s sculptures
take a long time to come together. &nbsp;He works

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:20.850
on these things for a number of years before
they kind of come together into a finished

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:26.679
piece. &nbsp;So our process was visiting him periodically
at his studio, checking in on what he was

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:32.670
working on, and trying to trace the development
of different pieces he was working on, so

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:37.600
we ended up with a lot of footage with him.
&nbsp;We shot at his studio. &nbsp;We also filmed

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.729
installations and openings of various pieces
and then there is a huge amount of archival

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:47.729
material, as well. &nbsp;He opened up his archives
to us. &nbsp; So we went back in there to not

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:52.049
only find documentation of his performance
works and his earlier works, but we also did

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:57.119
a lot of digging outside of that to find interviews
with Chris from earlier periods in his career

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:03.219
to try to really bring those time periods
to life, as well as other related material.

00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:09.569
&nbsp;It’s hard to give a specific number but
there was a lot [of footage] to work with.

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:14.709
&nbsp;Again, working with our editors and working
together to try to make sense of that and

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:20.890
to pick what the important parts were was
a big process. &nbsp;So we thought it went really

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.400
well. Film Courage: &nbsp;It was excellent (by
the way) seeing him [Chris Burden interviewed]

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.400
with Regis&nbsp;Philbin and it was interesting
to see both of those worlds contrast. &nbsp;I’m

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.653
wondering how [the two of you] viewing Chris
and his work has also shaped how you view

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:40.680
your work? How you view artists in general?
&nbsp;Because he was so different for his time

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.560
[has this allowed you] to feel freedom in
your own work? &nbsp;Or were you two just so enthralled

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.299
with Chris that you just focused [solely on
him]? &nbsp;Had it changed your view of art in

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:53.900
any way and what you do, [as a result of working
with him]? Richard Dewey: &nbsp;I think getting

00:02:53.900 --> 00:03:03.700
a first-hand view of his process was very
important and he was very patient. &nbsp;I think

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:09.890
an idea that came out a lot was that sometimes
with patience and a process, that takes a

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:18.210
long time. &nbsp;Things can come out of that where
you go down a path that’s unexpected, you

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:25.420
make a discovery that’s unexpected and that
happens over the course of years. &nbsp;So I think

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:30.980
my takeaway and influence from Chris’s work
is really just how important the process is

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:37.200
and that patience can really be a virtue in
art. Timothy Marrinan: &nbsp;I would absolutely

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.030
agree. &nbsp;That was an interesting thing to
learn about. &nbsp;And I guess one other thing

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:50.359
I found quite inspiring about Chris once we
got to know him and dig into his art was his

00:03:50.359 --> 00:03:54.560
commitment to the work. &nbsp;You know, whether
it was his early pieces where he was often

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.740
quite literally putting his body on the line
to explore an idea that was interesting to

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:04.790
him. &nbsp;Or his later pieces which I said can
take many years&nbsp;and a lot of investment&nbsp;of

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:09.459
both time and money to sort of bring together.
&nbsp;Often he started those pieces without a

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:14.579
clear idea of exactly where they would finish
or if they would end up in an institution

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:18.799
or bought by a collector or something like
that. &nbsp;He really had a lot of dedication

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:23.860
to his art, a really clear vision of what
he wanted to do (which he stuck to). &nbsp; And

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.759
that was interesting and inspiring, I think.
Film Courage: &nbsp;I have a quote from Chris

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:32.370
and he says “I don’t have to make an object
to have it be art. &nbsp;The performance itself

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:38.819
can be art.” &nbsp;So since Chris really&nbsp;lived
in public, would you say his whole life was

00:04:38.819 --> 00:04:46.400
an act of artistry in some sense? Richard
Dewey: &nbsp;I think it’s possible to make that

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:52.720
argument for the first part of his life but
I think there is a real line for the second

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:58.450
part of his life where that is not the case
and he became a very private person. &nbsp;There

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:05.620
was a real separation between his private
life and his public life and in his life in

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:12.180
the art world. &nbsp;And I think Chris really
valued that and I think the work benefited&nbsp;from

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:18.240
it as well. &nbsp;I don’t think doing the performances
he did and having the blur between his private

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.620
and public life was long-term sustainable
for him or anyone. Film Courage: &nbsp;It was

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:27.949
interesting [in the film] to see him on his
[secluded] Topanga Canyon property and at

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:31.340
one point in the film it refers to the fact
that he didn’t want to leave as was alluded

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:39.410
to in the film and he felt safe there. Because
he was so polarizing to those in the art world,

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:43.380
do you think that is why he stayed there [close
to home] because he felt more protected? &nbsp;He

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:48.419
could control what happened to him?&nbsp; Timothy
Marrinan: &nbsp;I think it’s probably fair to

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:57.699
say or I would think that the way he did become
more private in the second half of his career

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:03.440
is probably a reaction to his time doing performances.
&nbsp;Whereas Rich said, there was this real blurred

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:09.370
line between his public life and his private
life. &nbsp;But I think that once he moved up

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:14.530
to Topanga, you know he bought some land up
there, in the late 70’s/early 80’s and

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:18.130
over time built up a studio and a home up
there. &nbsp;It was just a place where he was

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:23.479
really comfortable. &nbsp;And it was kind of his
place to experiment with new ideas. &nbsp;He was

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:28.210
always really interested in what the next
project was. &nbsp;He was focused on what he was

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:33.069
doing next and got so immersed in that and
excited by that. &nbsp;So I think it was a place

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:37.840
where he just felt really comfortable to experiment
with new art works and to pursue the things

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.479
that were most interesting to him. &nbsp;It was
a very comfortable and a creatively free place

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:48.850
for him. Film Courage: &nbsp;How would you compare
Chris’s life in the 1970’s to today’s

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:55.009
selfie culture and vloggers where we all (essentially)
in the modern world live in public and desire

00:06:55.009 --> 00:07:00.759
to do so? &nbsp;He was doing this all before it
became [everyone’s current way of living

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:04.099
in 2017]. Timothy Marrinan: &nbsp;It’s interesting.
&nbsp;Yes. &nbsp;I think that elements of what he

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:10.830
was doing then are precursor to what you see
now. &nbsp;He did some pieces where he took control

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:17.159
of TV in that he would put his artwork…he
would buy ad space on TV and broadcast his

00:07:17.159 --> 00:07:20.880
artworks there. &nbsp;You see one [spot] right
at the start of our film and that was really

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.949
exciting to him. You know, the idea that he
was sort of pushing his art into a non-art

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:31.139
context and reaching a new audience with it.
&nbsp;And I suppose in a lot of ways, what he

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:35.030
did then was kind of a precursor to what you
see in the culture more broadly now. Film

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:38.419
Courage: &nbsp;But he was also criticized for
that ad in some sense, wasn’t he? Timothy

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:43.780
Marrinan: &nbsp;Yeah, I think that people to a
certain extent felt slightly…well some people

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:47.169
felt a little bit attacked by it. &nbsp;That they
weren’t expecting to see this artwork and

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:49.789
then all the sudden they are watching TV and
this thing [advertisement] pops in their face.

00:07:49.789 --> 00:07:53.300
But I think he enjoyed that element of it
as well and that reaction to it.

